<%#
  Only reachable when the token was still live — the controller renders
  unsubscribed directly otherwise. This page itself does nothing: it is safe
  for a mail scanner to fetch, because fetching it cannot remove anyone. Only
  the button below, a real form POST rather than the GET that got here, does.

  A plain hand-written <form>, not button_to or form_with — same reason as
  _subscribe_form.html.erb: either helper embeds an authenticity token, which
  has to live in the session, which would issue this anonymous visitor a
  session cookie. Forgery protection is already skipped on the action this
  posts to (SubscribersController explains why), so there is nothing here for
  a token to protect.
%>
<%# Own address carries a bearer token — see marketing.html.erb's use of this flag. %>
<% content_for :credential_page, true %>
<% content_for :title, "Unsubscribe — Remindly" %>

<h1>Unsubscribe <%= @subscriber.email %>?</h1>

<p class="lead">
  You will stop hearing from Remindly — the monthly note and anything else on
  this list.
</p>

<form action="<%= unsubscribe_path(token: params[:token]) %>" method="post">
  <input type="hidden" name="_method" value="delete">
  <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Yes, unsubscribe me</button>
</form>
